<v SPEAKER_00>Good evening. You know me, I'm Frank or Coleo from old Trinity place. And I'm the one who is appealing the the data center ruling by the zoning officer in back in February calling data center a warehouse you you on the council have requested a process to go through not to cut myself short but but it but in any case you have requested the beginning of a process that i believe is its next step is that you're going to look at a draft ordinance on october 6th that's what i understand but but what i have here for you is something that could shortcut the process which is kind of our idea of what a draft ordinance would be to regulate data centers first thing let me talk about what a data center is a data center is a facility whose Its primary purpose is the centralized storage, processing, management, or dissemination of digital data, information, or applications. This includes, but is not limited to, server forms, cloud computing hubs, co-location centers, and telecommunication switching hubs. Data centers typically house large-scale computer systems and supporting infrastructure such as backup generators, cooling systems, and uninterruptible power supplies. That does not sound like a warehouse to me, I have to say. The uses that we would suggest are conditional uses for both the C3, CM, and SM, all three of those zones. What would be some of the restrictions? Well, going to page three, energy use. Data centers shall operate on 100% renewable energy for all electrical and cooling needs, either through on-site generation or verifiable power purchase agreements. Backup generators should meet or exceed EPA Tier 4 final admission standards, and the facility should not require upgrades to the grid, the municipal electric distribution infrastructure, unless all those upgrades are fully paid for by the applicant. Water and cooling systems, I'll only go over the first provision, all water used for cooling operations or operations shall be non-potable, and all discharges shall be treated to a level equal or better than the affluent standard of the city. Going on to, I'm doing this a little bit out of order, noise emissions should not exceed 50 decibels at any property line, and a period of public informational meeting shall be held. The city may require a community benefits agreement as part of it. And then finally, a decommissioning plan shall outline procedures for the safe removal of server infrastructure, hazardous materials, batteries, electronic waste, all that kind of stuff, backed up by a substantial performance bond in case they decide that the data center glut has gotten a hold and they just wanna throw the keys up on the roof and leave us to clean it up. Those are suggested ordinance capable of Mr. Dastra and others that we would like to be considered as part of the ultimate zoning ordinance amendment for data centers. Thank you.

